250 ms. Evaluated request input/output lengths match the native-cache, earlier append and CSR runs request by request.\\n\\nNew and prior native-cache manifests differ only in creation time. Runtime logs confirm raw prefill KV, compressed KV H2D sharing the weight stream, and final usable capacity of 16,992 blocks (2,432 dedicated, 128 physical tail rows, 14,560 offloadable). The initial allocator estimate of 17,539 is subsequently reduced to the same 16,992 scheduler capacity; it is not an actual capacity difference.\\n\\n## Interpretation\\n\\nThere is no clear latency improvement in this run. SLO counts increased by six and three requests, while mean TPOT is essentially unchanged. The three recent variants average 172.376 ms (append), 172.318 ms (native batching/cache), and 172.397 ms (bundles). This does not establish a statistically significant improvement or regression, or demonstrate which component limits serving.\\n\\nEarlier CSR reference `20260915T051215Z` achieved 82.10% / 91.70% SLO and mean TPOT 167.822 ms. The latest run remains 1.40 / 0.30 percentage points below that reference and about 4.575 ms higher in mean TPOT. The CSR reference uses commit 30cc4f7 rather than b467dce plus local changes, including a different committed decoder implementation and a small capacity difference, so this is contextual rather than an isolated storage-only A/B.\\n\\nThe latest run does not record per-load internal copy counts, H2D windows, actual submitted payload/metadata bytes, bundle cache rates, worker waits, or compaction work. It cannot distinguish limited coalescing under actual serving selections from transfer savings being hidden or offset by other work. The dense correctness case from implementation is not evidence of serving copy counts or latency.\\n\\n## Artifact identity and limits\\n\\nAll ten currently modified source-file hashes still match the saved transfer-bundle artifact. The current g16/t128 extension contains the new bundle constructor/properties/checks. Its binary SHA-256 differs from the implementation-time snapshot and its mtime is 19:20:39 +0900, shortly before the server launch; the current hash is saved here. The source remains identical, but the compiled binary is not byte-identical to the previously validated build. The run manifest does not archive a loaded-binary hash or effective bundle setting, and the intended warmup logger line is absent from the saved server logs. Current code defaults to eight segments per bundle and the recorded launch command contains no bundle override. These observations support use of the new implementation but do not constitute a historical loaded-artifact/effective-setting attestation.\\n\\nNo source changes, test reruns, benchmarks, or serving experiments were performed during this results inspection. Recommended next diagnosis is a short serving-path measurement of actual copy counts, preparation/worker/collection time, H2D and narrowly scoped restore timing before making another layout change.\\n\\nOriginal summary: `/root/projects/tiered-serve/logs/serving/llama_3_1_70b_instruct/cao-cd3-fused/20260915_192116/summary.csv`.\\nOriginal request JSON: `/root/projects/tiered-serve/benchmark_results/serving_suite/20260915T102009Z/r01-cd3-kvcomp-c8192-q1p2-g16/client/rate_1.2.json`.\\n# CD3 KV transfer bundles\\n\\nImplemented on the native batching + segment-cache working tree based on `b467dce1717e4ec73b812347eb267cc9245e2cd7`. Changes are installed in `/root/projects/tiered-serve` inside `tiered-serve`, left uncommitted, and the normal base-rows-16 native extension has been rebuilt.\\n\\n## Behavior\\n\\n- Logical blocks and 128-block management segments retain independent retirement and versioning.\\n- By default, eight adjacent management segments share one bounded transfer bundle per K/V side. Their encoded records append consecutively, with 128-byte record alignment and one shared free tail. There is no worst-case reservation gap between member segments.\\n- Fresh publications and re-encoded records append to the common tail without moving earlier payload. Existing H2D plans retain private metadata and source ownership until their copies finish.\\n- When a bundle runs out of append capacity, the worker waits for the existing layer H2D event and packs only that bundle's current live records in segment/slot order. This also invalidates every member segment's physical-route version. Other bundles remain untouched.\\n- Freeing blocks changes their directories and segment versions. Physical hole reclamation is deferred until bundle capacity pressure; there is no per-load payload packing or immediate collection on every free.\\n- The transfer planner merges live records across management-segment boundaries. Each selected bundle normally produces one K span and one V span when its requested records are dense. Sparse or obsolete interior records split spans as needed to keep submitted bytes within the existing payload-plus-5% policy, including the 24-byte-per-block route table, and within existing GPU staging capacity. Unselected bundles send no payload.\\n- Unchanged segment route descriptions and unchanged bundle transfer descriptions are cached separately. A layer plan still assembles final device bases and private route metadata when its active set/generation changes.\\n- The bundle grouping is deterministic by neighboring logical slot groups. It does not infer request affinity or dynamically regroup segments based on serving traces.\\n\\n## Configuration and scope\\n\\n`TIERED_SERVE_KV_CD3_BUNDLE_SEGMENTS=8` is the default. Setting it to `1` at process startup keeps each management segment in its own bundle. The existing `TIERED_SERVE_KV_CD3_SEGMENT_BLOCKS=128` still controls management-segment size independently.\\n\\nOnly five files changed in this task: the CD3 KV Python backend, CD3 native host extension, existing CPU profile schema, existing KV diagnostic output, and focused KV tests. The CUDA decoder source, weight codec path, raw mixed/prefill load policy, shared-stream policy, separate K/V restore kernels, and persistent/GPU pool sizes are unchanged from the starting artifact.\\n\\nThe collection workspace is sized for one bundle per CPU worker and reused across layers. With the default 128 x 8 blocks and 32 KiB record capacity, its K/V total is at most 64 MiB per worker (previously 8 MiB), an increase of 56 MiB per worker. Encoding scratch remains segment-sized. Actual allocations are capped by host row count; physical workspace pages are touched when collection uses them. Bundle collection can move more data in one operation than the previous segment collection.\\n\\nThe existing KV benchmark now reports `bundle_segments` and `bundle_count`. The optional CPU load profile also exposes bundle cache hits/misses. These are not automatically exported by the separate default serving breakdown CSV.\\n\\n## Minimum verification\\n\\n- The normal native extension loader rebuilt and imported successfully; binary hash is in `native-binary.txt`.\\n- Ten focused KV cases passed through the existing production CPU encoder, append store, H2D submission and CUDA restoration paths.\\n- The new bundle lifecycle case uses 17 management segments across three bundles, including a short final bundle. Initial dense transfer submits exactly seven copy items (three K/V span pairs plus one route table), with submitted bytes equal to payload plus route metadata.\\n- That case also verifies immutable prior source bytes during append, segment-local cache invalidation, bundle cache reuse, selection changes, slot reuse, whole-bundle collection invalidation, skipping an unselected bundle, and bit-exact K/V restoration through both staging rings. Output rows omitted from the active set remain untouched.\\n- Existing affected cases cover one-segment bundle mode, stream switching and pressure collection, native segment reuse, Packed20 size changes, raw overflow, sparse gap budgets with near-raw GPU capacity, and asynchronous pending-block retirement with profiling.\\n- Python syntax and `git diff --check` passed.\\n\\nThe first verification batch passed eight cases and exposed an out-of-range ID in the new test fixture (ID 68 with a 68-entry ID table). The fixture's ID capacity was corrected; the affected bundle case and the pending-retirement/profile case then passed. No production defect was hidden or bypassed by this correction.\\n\\nNo serving experiment, latency benchmark, weight experiment or broad test suite was run. Copy coalescing and data integrity are verified; serving performance remains unmeasured.\\n\\n## Artifacts\\n\\n- `before/`, `after/`: full source snapshots of the ten previously modified tracked files.\\n- `implementation.patch`: this task's five-file delta.\\n- `working-tree.patch`: full uncommitted change versus HEAD.\\n- `files.json`: current source SHA-256 hashes.\\n- `build.log`, `tests.log`, `tests-affected.log`: build and focused verification evidence.\\n\\nUnrelated untracked diagnostic files were preserved. No commit, push, or server launch was performed.\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"i\":2,\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"0ae36c\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001344,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2554,\"output\":\"[req bench-921a9c57-1067] ttft=503.5ms tpot=136.8ms | slo200 att=75.9% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1099] ttft=393.1ms tpot=130.7ms | slo200 att=76.0% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1146] ttft=483.7ms tpot=144.6ms | slo200 att=76.0% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1100] ttft=493.4ms tpot=131.7ms | slo200 att=76.0% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1134] ttft=224.1ms tpot=148.2ms | slo200 att=76.0% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1163] ttft=288.7ms tpot=154.7ms | slo200 att=76.0% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1152] ttft=419.8ms tpot=138.8ms | slo200 att=76.1% | slo250 att=90.3%\\n[req bench-921a9c57-1104] ttft=401.0ms tpot=133.7ms | slo200 at